## First: load the environment (once per shell)

```bash
set -a; . {{ restic_config_dir }}/env; set +a    # then plain `restic ...` works
```
{% if _restic_secondary_enabled %}
For the **secondary** repo, source `env.secondary` instead — every command below then targets it:
```bash
set -a; . {{ restic_config_dir }}/env.secondary; set +a
```
{% endif %}

## Status

```bash
{% if restic_scheduler == "systemd" %}
systemctl list-timers 'restic-*'                       # next/last run of every job
journalctl --since '24 hours ago' -u 'restic-*'        # recent run output
journalctl -fu restic-backup.service                   # follow a live backup
{% else %}
cat /etc/cron.d/restic                                 # the schedule
ls -lt {{ restic_log_dir }}/                           # latest logs first
tail -F {{ restic_log_dir }}/*.log                     # follow live output
{% endif %}
restic snapshots --latest 5                            # 5 most recent backups
restic stats latest                                    # size & file count of latest
```

## Back up now

```bash
{% if restic_scheduler == "systemd" %}
systemctl start restic-backup.service && journalctl -fu restic-backup.service
{% else %}
{{ restic_config_dir }}/restic-backup.sh
{% endif %}
```

## Verify integrity

```bash
restic check                                           # fast, metadata only
restic check --read-data-subset=10%                    # slower, catches bit-rot
```

## Restore

**Step 1 — preview, writes nothing. Always run this first:**
```bash
restic restore latest --target /tmp/restore-preview --dry-run --verbose=2
```

**Step 2 — restore into a sandbox (never on top of live data), then copy out what you need:**
```bash
restic restore latest --target /var/restore                              # whole snapshot
restic restore latest --target /var/restore --include /etc/somefile      # one path
restic restore <snapshot-id> --target /var/restore                       # a specific snapshot (id from `restic snapshots`)
restic dump latest /etc/somefile > /tmp/somefile                         # stream one file, no temp tree
```

Browse the repo like a filesystem (read-only):
```bash
mkdir -p /mnt/restic && restic mount /mnt/restic &     # then ls /mnt/restic/snapshots/latest/
fusermount -u /mnt/restic                              # unmount when done
```

## Fix common problems

```bash
restic unlock                                          # clear a stale lock (only if no restic job is running)
sha256sum {{ restic_password_file }}                   # "wrong password" → confirm the password file is intact
restic forget --dry-run \
  --keep-daily {{ restic_keep_daily }} --keep-weekly {{ restic_keep_weekly }} --keep-monthly {{ restic_keep_monthly }} --keep-yearly {{ restic_keep_yearly }}   # preview what retention would delete
```

> Lost the password ⇒ the repo is **unrecoverable** (by design — no backdoor).
> See `restic_fetch_password_to_control` in the role README.

